1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,531
It is a quarter to seven in the morning,

2
00:00:02,551 --> 00:00:03,810
on the twenty-third of February,

3
00:00:03,830 --> 00:00:06,290
1945.

4
00:00:06,310 --> 00:00:09,689
On a field inside a prison camp in the

5
00:00:09,709 --> 00:00:11,578
Philippines, the off-duty Japanese guards

6
00:00:11,598 --> 00:00:14,222
are lining up for their morning exercises.

7
00:00:14,242 --> 00:00:16,111
They wear loincloths and sandals.

8
00:00:16,131 --> 00:00:19,530
Their rifles are locked in a room next door.

9
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:23,235
In the camp hospital, an American Navy

10
00:00:23,255 --> 00:00:24,638
nurse is watching them.

11
00:00:24,658 --> 00:00:26,392
Her name is Dorothy Still.

12
00:00:26,412 --> 00:00:29,198
She has been a prisoner for almost three

13
00:00:29,218 --> 00:00:30,952
years, and she is starving.

14
00:00:30,972 --> 00:00:34,109
She later wrote down what she said that morning.

15
00:00:34,129 --> 00:00:35,863
We could do calisthenics too,

16
00:00:35,883 --> 00:00:39,040
if we were as well fed as they are.

17
00:00:40,175 --> 00:00:42,955
A patient beside her notices something else.

18
00:00:42,975 --> 00:00:46,155
Columns of smoke are rising beyond the fence.

19
00:00:46,175 --> 00:00:48,555
Then comes the sound of engines,

20
00:00:48,575 --> 00:00:49,755
low and heavy.

21
00:00:49,775 --> 00:00:52,155
Nine American transport planes come in

22
00:00:52,175 --> 00:00:55,755
over the trees, only a few hundred feet up.

23
00:00:55,775 --> 00:00:59,375
And the sky beside the camp fills with parachutes.

24
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:03,281
The guards have been caught at the one

25
00:01:03,301 --> 00:01:05,982
moment of the day when they have no

26
00:01:06,002 --> 00:01:07,332
weapons in their hands.

27
00:01:07,352 --> 00:01:08,683
That is not luck.

28
00:01:08,703 --> 00:01:11,046
Someone told the Americans the exact minute.

29
00:01:11,066 --> 00:01:13,747
He had crawled out under the barbed wire

30
00:01:13,767 --> 00:01:14,780
five nights earlier.

31
00:01:16,425 --> 00:01:20,685
This is the true story of the raid at Los Baños.

32
00:01:20,705 --> 00:01:23,607
Inside the camp were two thousand one

33
00:01:23,627 --> 00:01:26,529
hundred and forty-seven people on a typed

34
00:01:26,549 --> 00:01:26,947
roll:

35
00:01:26,967 --> 00:01:28,199
Americans, Britons, Canadians,

36
00:01:28,219 --> 00:01:30,286
Australians, Dutch, Poles and Norwegians.

37
00:01:30,306 --> 00:01:32,374
Missionaries and nuns, mothers and

38
00:01:32,394 --> 00:01:34,043
children, eleven Navy nurses,

39
00:01:34,063 --> 00:01:36,548
and a baby three days old.

40
00:01:36,568 --> 00:01:39,470
The camp was twenty-five miles behind the

41
00:01:39,490 --> 00:01:40,325
Japanese lines.

42
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:44,233
The version you may have heard ends at

43
00:01:44,253 --> 00:01:46,467
nine in the morning, with the cheering.

44
00:01:46,487 --> 00:01:48,062
The documented story goes on.

45
00:01:48,082 --> 00:01:50,934
The man who really ran the camp got away,

46
00:01:50,954 --> 00:01:53,487
and for decades the books gave the wrong

47
00:01:53,507 --> 00:01:54,764
date for his death.

48
00:01:54,784 --> 00:01:57,317
The plaque at the camp today tells a

49
00:01:57,337 --> 00:01:59,231
different story about who freed it.

50
00:01:59,251 --> 00:02:02,104
And the town around the camp paid a price

51
00:02:02,124 --> 00:02:03,400
the celebrations left out.

52
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:07,640
Some of it comes from Army reports and a

53
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:08,660
war crimes trial.

54
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:11,720
Some comes from the memory of a single internee.

55
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:14,440
When a scene rests on one person's memory,

56
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:16,160
we will tell you whose.

57
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:19,651
The story starts in Manila,

58
00:02:19,671 --> 00:02:22,740
in January 1942.

59
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:25,389
Japanese troops had taken the city a few

60
00:02:25,409 --> 00:02:27,707
weeks after Pearl Harbor, and thousands of

61
00:02:27,727 --> 00:02:29,693
American and Allied civilians were locked

62
00:02:29,713 --> 00:02:31,700
inside the University of Santo Tomas.

63
00:02:33,150 --> 00:02:36,022
By 1943, Santo Tomas was overcrowded.

64
00:02:36,042 --> 00:02:38,842
In May, the Japanese sent eight hundred

65
00:02:38,862 --> 00:02:42,066
able-bodied men south to build a second camp.

66
00:02:42,086 --> 00:02:45,289
The site was the College of Agriculture of

67
00:02:45,309 --> 00:02:47,304
the University of the Philippines,

68
00:02:47,324 --> 00:02:50,928
at Los Baños, about forty miles from Manila.

69
00:02:50,948 --> 00:02:53,110
It sat between a wide lake,

70
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:56,210
Laguna de Bay, and the green slopes of an

71
00:02:56,230 --> 00:02:58,150
old volcano, Mount Makiling.

72
00:02:58,850 --> 00:03:02,350
One of the eight hundred was Ben Edwards,

73
00:03:02,370 --> 00:03:05,430
a young mechanic from Pan American Airways.

74
00:03:05,450 --> 00:03:06,750
Remember his name.

75
00:03:06,770 --> 00:03:09,830
In 1944, women and children were trucked

76
00:03:09,850 --> 00:03:12,710
down too, and by early 1945 there were

77
00:03:12,730 --> 00:03:14,970
more than two thousand people behind the wire.

78
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:18,727
About three hundred Catholic priests,

79
00:03:18,747 --> 00:03:21,592
brothers and nuns lived together in one

80
00:03:21,612 --> 00:03:24,049
section, which everyone called Vatican City.

81
00:03:24,069 --> 00:03:28,142
The rest of the camp had a name for their

82
00:03:28,163 --> 00:03:28,961
side too.

83
00:03:28,981 --> 00:03:29,800
Hell's Half-Acre.

84
00:03:30,500 --> 00:03:33,037
Among them were eleven women of the Navy

85
00:03:33,057 --> 00:03:35,274
Nurse Corps, under Chief Nurse Laura Cobb,

86
00:03:35,294 --> 00:03:36,553
who ran the hospital.

87
00:03:36,573 --> 00:03:38,151
And there was Frank Buckles,

88
00:03:38,171 --> 00:03:40,389
a veteran of the First World War.

89
00:03:40,409 --> 00:03:42,946
He would lose fifty pounds in this camp,

90
00:03:42,966 --> 00:03:45,503
and live to be a hundred and ten,

91
00:03:45,523 --> 00:03:47,760
the last American veteran of that war.

92
00:03:49,025 --> 00:03:51,539
According to the military historian Gordon

93
00:03:51,559 --> 00:03:53,651
Rottman, the first two Japanese

94
00:03:53,671 --> 00:03:54,918
commandants were lenient.

95
00:03:54,938 --> 00:03:58,912
Then, in July 1944, the camp got a new

96
00:03:58,932 --> 00:04:00,525
commandant, and a new supply officer.

97
00:04:02,250 --> 00:04:05,590
The commandant was Major Iwanaka.

98
00:04:05,610 --> 00:04:08,688
The internees remembered him as an old man

99
00:04:08,708 --> 00:04:11,785
who spent his days in a kimono and

100
00:04:11,805 --> 00:04:12,947
sandals, potting geraniums,

101
00:04:12,967 --> 00:04:14,883
painting watercolors and writing haiku.

102
00:04:14,903 --> 00:04:17,593
Some of them thought he was senile.

103
00:04:17,613 --> 00:04:21,078
He was never known to overrule his supply officer.

104
00:04:21,098 --> 00:04:24,176
And so, in practice, the camp belonged to

105
00:04:24,196 --> 00:04:24,996
that officer.

106
00:04:26,350 --> 00:04:28,870
His name was Sadaaki Konishi,

107
00:04:28,890 --> 00:04:29,967
a warrant officer.

108
00:04:29,987 --> 00:04:31,796
He was twenty-eight years old,

109
00:04:31,816 --> 00:04:34,550
five foot four, a farmer from Fukuoka

110
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:35,877
before the war.

111
00:04:35,897 --> 00:04:38,531
And he controlled the camp's food.

112
00:04:38,551 --> 00:04:41,185
Years later, an internee named Robert

113
00:04:41,205 --> 00:04:43,838
Wheeler described him in four words.

114
00:04:43,858 --> 00:04:46,070
A sadist, and terribly mean.

115
00:04:47,375 --> 00:04:49,498
Internees told the historian Bruce

116
00:04:49,518 --> 00:04:51,841
Henderson that Konishi once let starving

117
00:04:51,861 --> 00:04:54,281
men carry hundred-pound sacks of rice into

118
00:04:54,301 --> 00:04:56,721
the camp, for more than an hour.

119
00:04:56,741 --> 00:04:59,858
Then he ordered them to carry every sack back.

120
00:04:59,878 --> 00:05:02,298
What is documented is what happened to

121
00:05:02,318 --> 00:05:03,118
their food.

122
00:05:04,325 --> 00:05:06,494
The camp doctor, Dana Nance,

123
00:05:06,514 --> 00:05:07,369
kept count.

124
00:05:07,389 --> 00:05:11,182
In October 1944, each internee was getting

125
00:05:11,202 --> 00:05:13,279
eight hundred and eighty-one calories a

126
00:05:13,299 --> 00:05:16,423
day, almost two thirds less than the month before.

127
00:05:16,443 --> 00:05:19,218
On the first of November, Nance wrote to

128
00:05:19,238 --> 00:05:20,985
the commandant, in capital letters.

129
00:05:22,300 --> 00:05:24,426
The camp urgently needs more food.

130
00:05:24,446 --> 00:05:26,573
It did not get more food.

131
00:05:26,593 --> 00:05:28,719
After the war, Army investigators found

132
00:05:28,739 --> 00:05:31,223
that the ration fell from about four

133
00:05:31,243 --> 00:05:34,442
hundred grams of food a day per person in

134
00:05:34,462 --> 00:05:37,710
September 1944, to a hundred and fifty

135
00:05:37,730 --> 00:05:41,260
grams or less by February 1945.

136
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:43,700
That is about five ounces of food a day.

137
00:05:45,375 --> 00:05:49,073
At Thanksgiving 1944, Red Cross packages

138
00:05:49,093 --> 00:05:51,430
for the internees were held back.

139
00:05:51,450 --> 00:05:53,636
They remembered Konishi telling their

140
00:05:53,656 --> 00:05:54,006
committee:

141
00:05:54,026 --> 00:05:56,598
Before I'm done, you'll be eating dirt.

142
00:05:56,618 --> 00:06:00,302
Someone put up a sign on a patch of lawn.

143
00:06:00,322 --> 00:06:01,043
It said:

144
00:06:01,063 --> 00:06:02,524
Keep off the grass.

145
00:06:02,544 --> 00:06:03,655
We eat it.

146
00:06:04,825 --> 00:06:06,478
They did eat it.

147
00:06:06,498 --> 00:06:09,823
They ate weeds, and they ate the camp's

148
00:06:09,843 --> 00:06:11,078
cats and dogs.

149
00:06:11,098 --> 00:06:12,750
In late November, Dr.

150
00:06:12,770 --> 00:06:15,505
Nance went to Konishi.

151
00:06:15,525 --> 00:06:17,713
If you fail to remedy this situation,

152
00:06:17,733 --> 00:06:20,868
he told him, it can only mean that you are

153
00:06:20,888 --> 00:06:22,445
deliberately adopting a starvation policy.

154
00:06:22,465 --> 00:06:26,143
Konishi's answer, as Nance remembered it,

155
00:06:26,163 --> 00:06:27,318
was four words.

156
00:06:27,338 --> 00:06:28,905
Nothing can be done.

157
00:06:30,175 --> 00:06:33,412
By the start of 1945, people were dying

158
00:06:33,432 --> 00:06:36,642
every week, and the camp had to double its

159
00:06:36,662 --> 00:06:37,001
gravediggers.

160
00:06:37,021 --> 00:06:38,795
A girl named Sally Bateman,

161
00:06:38,815 --> 00:06:41,307
about thirteen, said many years later that

162
00:06:41,327 --> 00:06:44,178
some people died with food under their beds.

163
00:06:44,198 --> 00:06:47,049
They had been saving it, in case worse

164
00:06:47,069 --> 00:06:48,126
days were coming.

165
00:06:48,146 --> 00:06:50,638
Others collected recipes, for the day they

166
00:06:50,658 --> 00:06:51,735
would get out.

167
00:06:53,350 --> 00:06:56,981
Then, in January 1945, the Americans were

168
00:06:57,001 --> 00:06:58,766
coming back to the Philippines.

169
00:06:58,786 --> 00:07:01,622
On the sixth of January, the guards burned

170
00:07:01,642 --> 00:07:02,335
their records.

171
00:07:02,355 --> 00:07:04,834
The next morning, they handed the camp

172
00:07:04,854 --> 00:07:06,619
over to the internees' committee,

173
00:07:06,639 --> 00:07:07,710
and marched away.

174
00:07:09,025 --> 00:07:11,689
For one week, Los Baños was free.

175
00:07:11,709 --> 00:07:14,341
The internees raised the American and

176
00:07:14,361 --> 00:07:15,225
British flags.

177
00:07:15,245 --> 00:07:18,762
American Navy fighter planes flew over the camp.

178
00:07:18,782 --> 00:07:21,414
Then, on the fourteenth of January,

179
00:07:21,434 --> 00:07:23,182
the guards came back.

180
00:07:23,202 --> 00:07:25,392
The rations were cut again.

181
00:07:25,412 --> 00:07:28,045
And the commandant gave an order.

182
00:07:28,065 --> 00:07:31,139
Anyone caught outside the fence would be

183
00:07:31,159 --> 00:07:32,485
shot on sight.

184
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:35,913
Hungry men kept slipping out anyway,

185
00:07:35,933 --> 00:07:38,402
to buy food in the villages nearby.

186
00:07:38,422 --> 00:07:40,179
On the twentieth of January,

187
00:07:40,199 --> 00:07:43,023
an internee named Pat Hell was found shot

188
00:07:43,043 --> 00:07:45,156
about thirty feet outside the fence.

189
00:07:45,176 --> 00:07:48,020
He was still holding a scrawny dead chicken.

190
00:07:49,400 --> 00:07:52,140
Eight days later, at about a quarter to

191
00:07:52,160 --> 00:07:54,210
seven in the morning, guards caught

192
00:07:54,230 --> 00:07:55,590
another internee, George Louis,

193
00:07:55,610 --> 00:07:57,315
crawling back under the wire.

194
00:07:57,335 --> 00:07:59,848
At Konishi's trial, a witness testified

195
00:07:59,868 --> 00:08:02,229
that Konishi handed his own pistol to a

196
00:08:02,249 --> 00:08:04,060
soldier, who shot Louis in the head.

197
00:08:04,080 --> 00:08:06,770
Konishi denied it.

198
00:08:06,790 --> 00:08:11,060
In May 1945, an Army photographer took a

199
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:12,468
picture of the grave.

200
00:08:12,488 --> 00:08:14,932
The caption calls him an American civilian

201
00:08:14,952 --> 00:08:16,360
whom the Japanese killed.

202
00:08:17,750 --> 00:08:20,636
Around that time, the guards began digging

203
00:08:20,656 --> 00:08:22,712
long trenches near the barracks.

204
00:08:22,732 --> 00:08:25,202
Some internees thought they were graves.

205
00:08:25,222 --> 00:08:26,863
Nobody knows for certain.

206
00:08:26,883 --> 00:08:30,184
But a rumor spread that the Japanese meant

207
00:08:30,204 --> 00:08:33,110
to kill everyone before the Americans arrived.

208
00:08:34,825 --> 00:08:38,145
The internees were not as alone as they thought.

209
00:08:38,165 --> 00:08:40,743
Outside the wire, the hills and villages

210
00:08:40,763 --> 00:08:44,014
around Los Baños were full of Filipino guerrillas.

211
00:08:44,034 --> 00:08:47,345
There were the Hunters ROTC,

212
00:08:47,365 --> 00:08:49,525
a resistance group founded by military

213
00:08:49,545 --> 00:08:53,725
cadets and students in Manila in 1942.

214
00:08:53,745 --> 00:08:55,473
There were President Quezon's Own

215
00:08:55,493 --> 00:08:56,697
Guerrillas, Marking's Guerrillas,

216
00:08:56,717 --> 00:08:59,553
the Huks, and a Chinese Filipino squadron.

217
00:08:59,573 --> 00:09:02,409
For months, their scouts had been watching

218
00:09:02,429 --> 00:09:03,245
the camp.

219
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:06,844
One of the leaders of the Hunters was

220
00:09:06,864 --> 00:09:09,000
Gustavo Ingles.

221
00:09:09,020 --> 00:09:12,731
Arrested in 1943, he was tortured at Fort

222
00:09:12,751 --> 00:09:15,168
Santiago and sentenced to thirteen years.

223
00:09:15,188 --> 00:09:18,465
In June 1944, the Hunters raided the

224
00:09:18,485 --> 00:09:20,435
prison where he was held, and broke him

225
00:09:20,455 --> 00:09:21,440
out with fifty others.

226
00:09:22,775 --> 00:09:25,395
On the twelfth of February 1945,

227
00:09:25,415 --> 00:09:28,295
a young internee named Freddy Zervoulakos,

228
00:09:28,315 --> 00:09:30,529
the son of a Greek father and a Filipino

229
00:09:30,549 --> 00:09:34,155
mother, slipped out of the camp and met Ingles.

230
00:09:34,175 --> 00:09:36,975
The guerrillas wanted to get the internees out.

231
00:09:36,995 --> 00:09:39,815
But they needed the camp's leaders to agree.

232
00:09:41,525 --> 00:09:43,353
The answer was no.

233
00:09:43,373 --> 00:09:45,663
The committee's leader, George Gray,

234
00:09:45,683 --> 00:09:47,975
told Ingles that under international law,

235
00:09:47,995 --> 00:09:49,970
they were a civilian internment camp.

236
00:09:49,990 --> 00:09:52,298
And he asked the question that nobody

237
00:09:52,318 --> 00:09:52,963
could answer.

238
00:09:52,983 --> 00:09:55,624
Did the guerrillas have any way to move

239
00:09:55,644 --> 00:09:57,287
more than two thousand people,

240
00:09:57,307 --> 00:10:00,280
many of them sick and too weak to walk,

241
00:10:00,300 --> 00:10:02,940
out of enemy territory and across to the

242
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:03,760
American lines?

243
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:06,000
They did not.

244
00:10:06,020 --> 00:10:08,380
Only the American Army had the planes,

245
00:10:08,400 --> 00:10:11,100
the boats and the guns, and it was

246
00:10:11,120 --> 00:10:13,140
twenty-five miles away, fighting for Manila.

247
00:10:13,160 --> 00:10:15,860
Somebody would have to get through to the

248
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:17,900
Americans, and tell them everything about

249
00:10:17,920 --> 00:10:18,720
the camp.

250
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:22,651
On the night of the eighteenth of

251
00:10:22,671 --> 00:10:24,684
February, three men decided to go.

252
00:10:24,704 --> 00:10:27,780
One was Freddy Zervoulakos.

253
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:29,535
The second was Ben Edwards,

254
00:10:29,555 --> 00:10:30,939
the Pan Am mechanic.

255
00:10:30,959 --> 00:10:33,397
The third was Pete Miles, an engineer,

256
00:10:33,417 --> 00:10:35,503
a rough-and-tumble Texan in his early

257
00:10:35,523 --> 00:10:37,961
thirties, who had once been sent to

258
00:10:37,981 --> 00:10:41,140
Sumatra by the Saint Louis Zoo to catch animals.

259
00:10:42,525 --> 00:10:44,667
Ben Edwards described the escape years

260
00:10:44,687 --> 00:10:47,550
later, and Henderson tells it from his account.

261
00:10:47,570 --> 00:10:50,792
At nine at night, the three men walked out

262
00:10:50,812 --> 00:10:52,954
of their barracks carrying a lantern.

263
00:10:52,974 --> 00:10:55,477
Miles limped and groaned between the other

264
00:10:55,497 --> 00:10:57,278
two, pretending to be ill.

265
00:10:57,298 --> 00:10:59,080
They walked toward the hospital,

266
00:10:59,100 --> 00:11:01,602
then crawled under the wire behind it.

267
00:11:01,622 --> 00:11:04,865
A manned guard post stood about forty feet away.

268
00:11:06,125 --> 00:11:08,587
Edwards kicked an empty tin can.

269
00:11:08,607 --> 00:11:11,069
The noise, he remembered, was deafening.

270
00:11:11,089 --> 00:11:11,897
Nobody came.

271
00:11:11,917 --> 00:11:15,206
The three men reached a guerrilla camp in

272
00:11:15,226 --> 00:11:16,033
the hills.

273
00:11:16,053 --> 00:11:18,516
And the next morning, Filipino boatmen

274
00:11:18,536 --> 00:11:21,825
took Pete Miles across the lake in a

275
00:11:21,845 --> 00:11:23,895
banca, a narrow outrigger canoe,

276
00:11:23,915 --> 00:11:25,465
toward the American lines.

277
00:11:27,225 --> 00:11:28,697
On the nineteenth of February,

278
00:11:28,717 --> 00:11:30,787
Miles was brought to the headquarters of

279
00:11:30,807 --> 00:11:33,845
the 11th Airborne Division at Parañaque,

280
00:11:33,865 --> 00:11:35,013
south of Manila.

281
00:11:35,033 --> 00:11:36,570
The division's intelligence officer,

282
00:11:36,590 --> 00:11:38,127
Lieutenant Colonel Henry Muller,

283
00:11:38,147 --> 00:11:41,630
had been trying to plan a rescue from a

284
00:11:41,650 --> 00:11:42,409
rough map.

285
00:11:42,429 --> 00:11:43,576
Miles corrected it.

286
00:11:43,596 --> 00:11:46,301
He marked the quarters of the internees,

287
00:11:46,321 --> 00:11:49,415
the guard houses, the sentries and the pillboxes.

288
00:11:49,435 --> 00:11:52,140
He even estimated how many people would

289
00:11:52,160 --> 00:11:54,885
have to be carried out on stretchers.

290
00:11:56,200 --> 00:12:00,225
Then Muller asked him about the guards' routine.

291
00:12:00,245 --> 00:12:02,248
Miles mentioned their exercises.

292
00:12:02,268 --> 00:12:05,282
Muller later told Henderson how the

293
00:12:05,302 --> 00:12:06,293
conversation went.

294
00:12:06,313 --> 00:12:07,304
Every morning?

295
00:12:07,324 --> 00:12:10,844
Yes, and always at the same time.

296
00:12:10,864 --> 00:12:11,855
What time?

297
00:12:11,875 --> 00:12:15,920
They start exercising at a quarter to seven.

298
00:12:17,250 --> 00:12:19,922
Muller moved the attack from eight in the

299
00:12:19,942 --> 00:12:22,613
morning to seven, when the guards would be

300
00:12:22,633 --> 00:12:24,295
fifteen minutes into their exercises,

301
00:12:24,315 --> 00:12:25,978
with their rifles locked away.

302
00:12:25,998 --> 00:12:28,333
The division's history said of Pete Miles:

303
00:12:28,353 --> 00:12:30,351
The assistance which this brave man

304
00:12:30,371 --> 00:12:32,390
rendered cannot be too highly praised.

305
00:12:34,175 --> 00:12:36,026
The division's commander, Major General

306
00:12:36,046 --> 00:12:39,020
Joseph Swing, did not have a spare regiment.

307
00:12:39,040 --> 00:12:42,387
So the plan his staff drew up was small,

308
00:12:42,407 --> 00:12:44,258
fast and complicated, and everything

309
00:12:44,278 --> 00:12:45,775
depended on one minute.

310
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:48,435
It had four parts.

311
00:12:48,455 --> 00:12:49,525
The reconnaissance platoon,

312
00:12:49,545 --> 00:12:52,435
about thirty men, would cross the lake at

313
00:12:52,455 --> 00:12:55,707
night and creep up to the fence with the

314
00:12:55,727 --> 00:12:56,071
guerrillas.

315
00:12:56,091 --> 00:12:57,525
One company of paratroopers,

316
00:12:57,545 --> 00:13:00,450
B Company of the 511th Parachute Infantry,

317
00:13:00,470 --> 00:13:03,645
would jump onto a field next to the camp.

318
00:13:03,665 --> 00:13:06,130
The rest of their battalion would cross

319
00:13:06,150 --> 00:13:07,905
the lake in fifty-four amphibious

320
00:13:07,925 --> 00:13:08,970
tractors, called amtracs.

321
00:13:08,990 --> 00:13:11,455
And a column with artillery and tank

322
00:13:11,475 --> 00:13:13,585
destroyers would attack down the highway,

323
00:13:13,605 --> 00:13:15,380
to keep the Japanese busy.

324
00:13:16,725 --> 00:13:18,925
The guerrillas, mostly men of the Hunters

325
00:13:18,945 --> 00:13:22,562
ROTC, would wait in the grass with the

326
00:13:22,582 --> 00:13:24,819
American scouts and kill the sentries the

327
00:13:24,839 --> 00:13:26,108
moment the attack began.

328
00:13:26,128 --> 00:13:28,385
Other guerrilla units would block the roads.

329
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:32,166
Swing's staff estimated that between eight

330
00:13:32,186 --> 00:13:34,238
thousand and fifteen thousand Japanese

331
00:13:34,258 --> 00:13:37,139
soldiers were within four hours' march of

332
00:13:37,159 --> 00:13:37,968
the camp.

333
00:13:37,988 --> 00:13:40,039
Henderson writes that the planners

334
00:13:40,059 --> 00:13:42,960
expected casualties as high as thirty percent.

335
00:13:44,275 --> 00:13:46,691
We are about halfway through the story of

336
00:13:46,711 --> 00:13:48,635
Los Baños.

337
00:13:48,655 --> 00:13:51,104
If you want more prisoner of war stories

338
00:13:51,124 --> 00:13:53,755
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

339
00:13:53,775 --> 00:13:54,575
Frontlines.

340
00:13:56,025 --> 00:13:57,875
On the twenty-first of February,

341
00:13:57,895 --> 00:13:59,745
a guerrilla commander, Colonel Romeo

342
00:13:59,765 --> 00:14:02,572
Espino, radioed the Americans that the

343
00:14:02,592 --> 00:14:05,463
Japanese had Los Baños scheduled for massacre.

344
00:14:05,483 --> 00:14:08,711
No proof has ever been found that a

345
00:14:08,731 --> 00:14:09,929
massacre was planned.

346
00:14:09,949 --> 00:14:12,385
But nobody could take that chance.

347
00:14:13,675 --> 00:14:15,860
That night, in a storm, the reconnaissance

348
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:19,055
platoon crossed the lake in bancas.

349
00:14:19,075 --> 00:14:21,035
On the far shore, at a schoolhouse in the

350
00:14:21,055 --> 00:14:23,239
village of Nanhaya, they met the

351
00:14:23,259 --> 00:14:25,300
guerrillas, and they met Ben Edwards and

352
00:14:25,320 --> 00:14:27,615
Freddy Zervoulakos.

353
00:14:27,635 --> 00:14:29,908
Edwards drew the camp on the blackboard.

354
00:14:29,928 --> 00:14:32,875
Both men would go back in with the attack.

355
00:14:34,175 --> 00:14:36,107
On the night of the twenty-second,

356
00:14:36,127 --> 00:14:37,408
the teams set out.

357
00:14:37,428 --> 00:14:38,709
There was no moon.

358
00:14:38,729 --> 00:14:40,986
For about seven hours they waded through

359
00:14:41,006 --> 00:14:42,937
flooded rice paddies, until they were

360
00:14:42,957 --> 00:14:45,214
lying in the grass near the fence.

361
00:14:45,234 --> 00:14:47,491
Some of them, according to the division

362
00:14:47,511 --> 00:14:50,093
history, were as close as three feet from

363
00:14:50,113 --> 00:14:52,715
the sentry they had been assigned to kill.

364
00:14:54,025 --> 00:14:56,116
At Nichols Field, the paratroopers slept

365
00:14:56,136 --> 00:14:58,227
under the wings of their planes.

366
00:14:58,247 --> 00:15:00,689
And at about midnight, a report reached

367
00:15:00,709 --> 00:15:01,393
General Swing.

368
00:15:01,413 --> 00:15:03,855
Night fighter pilots said they had seen

369
00:15:03,875 --> 00:15:06,318
hundreds of trucks with their lights on,

370
00:15:06,338 --> 00:15:07,745
driving into the camp.

371
00:15:09,050 --> 00:15:11,757
If it was true, the paratroopers would be

372
00:15:11,777 --> 00:15:13,462
jumping onto a reinforced garrison.

373
00:15:13,482 --> 00:15:15,507
Swing decided to ignore the report.

374
00:15:15,527 --> 00:15:18,575
He did not pass it on to the men.

375
00:15:18,595 --> 00:15:21,644
At one in the morning, he drove down to

376
00:15:21,664 --> 00:15:24,050
the beach to watch the amtracs leave.

377
00:15:25,750 --> 00:15:28,509
At a quarter past five, the amtracs moved

378
00:15:28,529 --> 00:15:30,247
out onto the dark lake.

379
00:15:30,267 --> 00:15:33,026
Soon after six, nine transport planes took off.

380
00:15:33,046 --> 00:15:35,111
And at a quarter to seven,

381
00:15:35,131 --> 00:15:37,910
the guards walked out onto the exercise field.

382
00:15:39,225 --> 00:15:40,934
A few minutes before seven,

383
00:15:40,954 --> 00:15:43,009
a sentry saw something and fired.

384
00:15:43,029 --> 00:15:45,776
The scouts and the guerrillas did not wait.

385
00:15:45,796 --> 00:15:47,851
They opened fire a little early.

386
00:15:47,871 --> 00:15:50,272
A Filipino officer of the Hunters ordered

387
00:15:50,292 --> 00:15:52,693
a guard cut down with a bolo,

388
00:15:52,713 --> 00:15:53,731
a long machete.

389
00:15:53,751 --> 00:15:56,843
At two minutes to seven, smoke went up on

390
00:15:56,863 --> 00:15:59,264
the drop zone and on the beach,

391
00:15:59,284 --> 00:16:01,705
to guide the planes and the boats.

392
00:16:03,050 --> 00:16:05,109
At seven o'clock, the first parachute

393
00:16:05,129 --> 00:16:06,841
snapped open over the field.

394
00:16:06,861 --> 00:16:08,919
It belonged to the company commander,

395
00:16:08,939 --> 00:16:09,612
Lieutenant Ringler.

396
00:16:09,632 --> 00:16:12,037
They jumped from four or five hundred

397
00:16:12,057 --> 00:16:15,155
feet, low enough that each man was in the

398
00:16:15,175 --> 00:16:17,581
air for less than half a minute.

399
00:16:17,601 --> 00:16:20,698
In less than a minute, they were all on

400
00:16:20,718 --> 00:16:21,391
the ground.

401
00:16:21,411 --> 00:16:23,490
Not one of them was hurt.

402
00:16:24,750 --> 00:16:26,970
At the same moment, the first amtracs

403
00:16:26,990 --> 00:16:29,530
climbed out of the lake onto the beach,

404
00:16:29,550 --> 00:16:31,450
and the first American artillery shell

405
00:16:31,470 --> 00:16:33,690
landed on a hill to the north,

406
00:16:33,710 --> 00:16:35,290
where the diversion had begun.

407
00:16:35,310 --> 00:16:37,850
On the exercise field, the guards ran for

408
00:16:37,870 --> 00:16:40,090
the room where their rifles were locked.

409
00:16:40,110 --> 00:16:42,030
Many of them never got there.

410
00:16:43,225 --> 00:16:45,922
Inside the barracks, nobody knew what was

411
00:16:45,942 --> 00:16:46,310
happening.

412
00:16:46,330 --> 00:16:49,026
Sally Bateman's mother told her to get

413
00:16:49,046 --> 00:16:50,191
under the bed.

414
00:16:50,211 --> 00:16:52,907
Sally remembered what she saw from there.

415
00:16:52,927 --> 00:16:55,624
I looked out and saw paratrooper boots.

416
00:16:55,644 --> 00:16:58,729
It was the most beautiful sight you can

417
00:16:58,749 --> 00:16:59,549
ever imagine.

418
00:17:00,850 --> 00:17:03,301
A young internee named Jerry Sams,

419
00:17:03,321 --> 00:17:06,596
down to ninety pounds, climbed onto a roof

420
00:17:06,616 --> 00:17:09,479
and looked over at the exercise field.

421
00:17:09,499 --> 00:17:11,538
It was covered with bodies.

422
00:17:11,558 --> 00:17:14,421
In the women's barracks, an internee named

423
00:17:14,441 --> 00:17:17,303
Margie Whitaker asked a soldier if the

424
00:17:17,323 --> 00:17:18,539
Marines had landed.

425
00:17:18,559 --> 00:17:19,363
He said:

426
00:17:19,383 --> 00:17:20,186
Sorry, sister.

427
00:17:20,206 --> 00:17:21,030
Army paratroopers.

428
00:17:22,725 --> 00:17:24,167
Within about fifteen minutes,

429
00:17:24,187 --> 00:17:26,360
the paratroopers had reached the camp

430
00:17:26,380 --> 00:17:29,285
through gaps the scouts had cut in the

431
00:17:29,305 --> 00:17:31,478
fence, and the pillboxes were destroyed.

432
00:17:31,498 --> 00:17:33,671
Then the amtracs arrived, crashing through

433
00:17:33,691 --> 00:17:35,499
the wire and the bushes.

434
00:17:35,519 --> 00:17:38,057
The fighting in the camp was over.

435
00:17:38,077 --> 00:17:39,905
Now the hardest part began.

436
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:44,169
But the internees did not want to leave

437
00:17:44,189 --> 00:17:47,157
what little they owned, and many were too

438
00:17:47,177 --> 00:17:48,278
weak to hurry.

439
00:17:48,298 --> 00:17:50,146
The soldiers had no time.

440
00:17:50,166 --> 00:17:52,761
So they set the barracks on fire,

441
00:17:52,781 --> 00:17:54,255
to get people moving.

442
00:17:54,275 --> 00:17:56,497
An internee named Carol Talbot remembered

443
00:17:56,517 --> 00:17:57,244
the order.

444
00:17:57,264 --> 00:18:00,233
Lady, you've got five minutes to get into

445
00:18:00,253 --> 00:18:01,053
an amtrac.

446
00:18:01,700 --> 00:18:04,439
Among the people carried out was the baby,

447
00:18:04,459 --> 00:18:06,507
Lois McCoy, born three days before.

448
00:18:06,527 --> 00:18:09,956
A soldier carried her in the lining of his helmet.

449
00:18:09,976 --> 00:18:13,059
The sick and the old were lifted onto the

450
00:18:13,079 --> 00:18:14,093
amtracs on stretchers.

451
00:18:14,113 --> 00:18:16,162
By about a quarter to nine,

452
00:18:16,182 --> 00:18:18,921
the camp was empty, and the column was

453
00:18:18,941 --> 00:18:20,320
moving toward the beach.

454
00:18:21,625 --> 00:18:23,525
The battalion commander, Major Henry

455
00:18:23,545 --> 00:18:26,597
Burgess, chose to go back across the lake,

456
00:18:26,617 --> 00:18:27,749
in two trips.

457
00:18:27,769 --> 00:18:30,053
The first lift carried about fifteen

458
00:18:30,073 --> 00:18:30,821
hundred people.

459
00:18:30,841 --> 00:18:33,125
The others waited on the beach,

460
00:18:33,145 --> 00:18:36,581
under mortar fire, for the amtracs to come back.

461
00:18:36,601 --> 00:18:38,885
And at five o'clock, General Swing

462
00:18:38,905 --> 00:18:41,573
reported that two thousand one hundred and

463
00:18:41,593 --> 00:18:43,877
forty-seven people were safe behind the

464
00:18:43,897 --> 00:18:46,181
American lines, with one internee slightly

465
00:18:46,201 --> 00:18:47,001
wounded.

466
00:18:47,925 --> 00:18:50,145
The price was paid outside the camp.

467
00:18:50,165 --> 00:18:52,065
In the diversion to the north,

468
00:18:52,085 --> 00:18:55,207
in the hills at Lecheria, the captain

469
00:18:55,227 --> 00:18:57,551
commanding the tank destroyers was shot

470
00:18:57,571 --> 00:18:58,723
through the forehead.

471
00:18:58,743 --> 00:19:01,459
Two other American soldiers were killed in

472
00:19:01,479 --> 00:19:02,240
that fight.

473
00:19:02,260 --> 00:19:04,585
Two Filipino guerrillas of the Hunters,

474
00:19:04,605 --> 00:19:08,125
Atanacio Castillo and Anselmo Soler,

475
00:19:08,145 --> 00:19:09,105
were killed in the raid.

476
00:19:10,400 --> 00:19:13,460
How many Japanese died is less clear.

477
00:19:13,480 --> 00:19:16,851
Reports in 1945 said the guards had been

478
00:19:16,871 --> 00:19:19,686
killed to the last man, about two hundred

479
00:19:19,706 --> 00:19:21,103
and forty of them.

480
00:19:21,123 --> 00:19:23,583
Later historians put the number killed in

481
00:19:23,603 --> 00:19:25,709
the camp at seventy or eighty.

482
00:19:25,729 --> 00:19:28,543
And not everyone in the garrison was on

483
00:19:28,563 --> 00:19:30,314
the exercise field that morning.

484
00:19:30,334 --> 00:19:32,440
Two men in particular were not:

485
00:19:32,460 --> 00:19:34,940
the old commandant, and his supply officer.

486
00:19:36,275 --> 00:19:38,355
General Douglas MacArthur announced the

487
00:19:38,375 --> 00:19:40,035
rescue in these words.

488
00:19:40,055 --> 00:19:42,975
Nothing could be more satisfying to a

489
00:19:42,995 --> 00:19:45,075
soldier's heart than this rescue.

490
00:19:45,095 --> 00:19:47,615
God was certainly with us today.

491
00:19:49,300 --> 00:19:51,445
The next day, the twenty-fourth of

492
00:19:51,465 --> 00:19:54,331
February, the raid was on the front pages

493
00:19:54,351 --> 00:19:55,413
of American newspapers.

494
00:19:55,433 --> 00:19:58,660
One paper in Ohio put it in the same

495
00:19:58,680 --> 00:20:00,104
headline as another battle.

496
00:20:00,124 --> 00:20:02,629
American troops, it said, had freed two

497
00:20:02,649 --> 00:20:04,794
thousand one hundred and forty-six more

498
00:20:04,814 --> 00:20:07,700
prisoners, and broken the stalemate on Iwo Jima.

499
00:20:08,900 --> 00:20:11,387
On the same morning as the raid,

500
00:20:11,407 --> 00:20:14,610
far to the north, Marines on Iwo Jima had

501
00:20:14,630 --> 00:20:17,834
raised an American flag on top of Mount Suribachi.

502
00:20:17,854 --> 00:20:20,341
That afternoon, a second, larger flag went

503
00:20:20,361 --> 00:20:22,848
up, and a photographer named Joe Rosenthal

504
00:20:22,868 --> 00:20:24,639
took a picture of it.

505
00:20:24,659 --> 00:20:26,787
The photograph was published on Sunday,

506
00:20:26,807 --> 00:20:28,240
the twenty-fifth of February.

507
00:20:29,550 --> 00:20:32,618
It became the most famous image of the war.

508
00:20:32,638 --> 00:20:35,899
The raid at Los Baños was not hidden by

509
00:20:35,919 --> 00:20:36,261
censorship.

510
00:20:36,281 --> 00:20:39,159
It made the front pages for a day.

511
00:20:39,179 --> 00:20:41,694
Then the photograph from Iwo Jima took

512
00:20:41,714 --> 00:20:44,250
over, and the rescue faded from memory.

513
00:20:45,975 --> 00:20:48,095
For the people of Los Baños,

514
00:20:48,115 --> 00:20:51,152
the story did not end on the twenty-third.

515
00:20:51,172 --> 00:20:53,445
In the villages around the town,

516
00:20:53,465 --> 00:20:55,355
the killing had already started.

517
00:20:55,375 --> 00:20:57,266
Army war crimes investigators recorded

518
00:20:57,286 --> 00:20:59,559
that on the twenty-first of February,

519
00:20:59,579 --> 00:21:01,852
two days before the raid, Japanese

520
00:21:01,872 --> 00:21:04,144
soldiers killed sixty-five people from ten

521
00:21:04,164 --> 00:21:07,375
families in the village of Tadlak.

522
00:21:07,395 --> 00:21:09,302
On the twenty-second, they killed

523
00:21:09,322 --> 00:21:12,825
forty-five people from five families in Maahas.

524
00:21:13,525 --> 00:21:16,165
The Japanese commander in the region was

525
00:21:16,185 --> 00:21:17,785
Colonel Masatoshi Fujishige,

526
00:21:17,805 --> 00:21:20,370
and his war was against the guerrillas.

527
00:21:20,390 --> 00:21:23,025
A captured order, printed in the 11th

528
00:21:23,045 --> 00:21:24,625
Airborne's history in 1948,

529
00:21:24,645 --> 00:21:25,111
reads:

530
00:21:25,131 --> 00:21:27,543
Kill all American soldiers brutally.

531
00:21:27,563 --> 00:21:30,947
Kill all Filipinos who oppose our Emperor.

532
00:21:30,967 --> 00:21:34,351
And after the Americans left with the

533
00:21:34,371 --> 00:21:37,269
internees, Japanese soldiers came back to

534
00:21:37,289 --> 00:21:38,525
Los Baños.

535
00:21:39,225 --> 00:21:41,579
In the early hours of the twenty-seventh

536
00:21:41,599 --> 00:21:43,953
of February, a Japanese unit reached the

537
00:21:43,973 --> 00:21:46,327
college grounds, where the camp had stood.

538
00:21:46,347 --> 00:21:49,041
Its captain later said he told his men

539
00:21:49,061 --> 00:21:51,415
that everyone there was to be killed,

540
00:21:51,435 --> 00:21:54,128
because they believed it was a guerrilla area.

541
00:21:54,148 --> 00:21:56,502
More than sixty people had taken shelter

542
00:21:56,522 --> 00:21:58,877
in the Catholic church on the campus.

543
00:21:58,897 --> 00:22:01,590
The war crimes report says that those who

544
00:22:01,610 --> 00:22:03,645
refused to leave were burned inside.

545
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,590
The next day, Konishi was there.

546
00:22:07,610 --> 00:22:09,967
A Los Baños man, Fernando Bernardo,

547
00:22:09,987 --> 00:22:12,989
was ordered with six others to bury the dead.

548
00:22:13,009 --> 00:22:14,668
Among the bodies, he testified,

549
00:22:14,688 --> 00:22:17,354
